WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.470
Benjamin Cook: "Global warming
is caused by cow farts."

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:05.560
It's not by their farts,
but it's by belching.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.130
Cook: "A few degrees'
difference is not a big deal."

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:09.240
And the way I always like
to think about it too

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.730
is like your body's temperature.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:13.130
If your temperature is three
or four degrees warmer,

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:15.120
then you're seriously sick.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.020
"It's too late to do anything about it."

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.090
Unless you're Elon Musk

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.423
and gonna head off towards Mars,

00:00:20.423 --> 00:00:21.298
we're all stuck here,

00:00:21.298 --> 00:00:22.750
so we should try to figure out

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:24.940
how we can make it the best planet we can.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:26.082
I'm Deepti Singh.

00:00:26.082 --> 00:00:27.390
I'm an assistant professor

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:28.690
in the School of the Environment

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:30.510
at Washington State University.

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.340
I've been studying climate
change for about 11 years,

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.870
and I study extreme weather events

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:38.670
and how human activities
are influencing them.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.160
My name is Benjamin Cook,
and I'm a climate scientist

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.700
at the NASA Goddard
Institute for Space Studies.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.850
I've been working there
for about 14 years now.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:48.480
And I study how droughts are changing

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.193
with global warming and climate change.

00:00:50.193 --> 00:00:53.560
And today we'll be debunking
myths about global warming.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.170
Cook: Myths from pop culture.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:56.850
Oh, boy, I'm glad you
got this one, Deepti.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.380
"The sun is causing global warming."

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.640
Changes in the amount of
energy we get from the sun

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.630
do affect our climate.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.540
But over the last 150 years, we know that

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.670
because the amount of energy
we're getting from the sun

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.710
has not changed significantly
over this period.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:12.936
Satellites have been recording

00:01:12.936 --> 00:01:15.610
the amount of solar radiation
that our planet receives.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.623
I think Ben has a graph that shows that.

00:01:17.623 --> 00:01:19.200
Cook: And what we're looking
at here on the yellow

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.080
is the amount of energy
that's coming from the sun,

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.610
and red is global temperatures.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.310
It's pretty clear that
the amount of energy

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:27.930
we're getting from the sun
has been more or less flat

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.160
for the last several decades,

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.517
even as temperatures
continue to go up and up.

00:01:32.517 --> 00:01:33.975
Singh: "Scientists don't agree

00:01:33.975 --> 00:01:36.060
on what causes climate change."

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:39.685
100% of the climate scientists
on this Skype call agree.

00:01:39.685 --> 00:01:42.530
If you review the published literature

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.230
in reputable journals
by reputable scientists,

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:46.630
all those papers agree

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:49.350
that climate change is
caused by human activities.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:53.580
There's really no other
explanation that fits the data.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.070
We've looked at the sun.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:57.362
We've looked at just natural variations

00:01:57.362 --> 00:02:01.040
in circulation in the
ocean, in the atmosphere.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.390
We've looked at volcanoes.

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:04.710
We've looked at changes in ecosystems.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.900
And at the end of the day, the only thing

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:10.360
that can adequately explain
the degree of warming

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.880
that we've seen over the last 150 years

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.590
is human greenhouse gas emissions,

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:17.590
primarily through the
burning of fossil fuels.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:18.940
There's a real clear incentive

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.530
for people to find some other explanation.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:22.710
Nobody can come up with

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.502
even a plausible alternative hypothesis.

00:02:25.502 --> 00:02:27.943
"Global warming is caused by cow farts."

00:02:27.943 --> 00:02:30.910
It's not by their farts,
but it's by belching.

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:33.500
Agriculture is a pretty
substantial contributor

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.770
to greenhouse gas emissions, close to 25%.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.080
It's not the whole 25%,
but it's a good chunk.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.410
It's important to note,
too, that even the cow burps

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.080
that are producing this
methane is not natural.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.960
It's all part of a kind of
human agricultural system.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:48.670
So blaming it all on cows

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:50.514
doesn't take people off the hook.

00:02:50.514 --> 00:02:53.080
"Plants and animals will adapt."

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:54.610
So, we know that in the past,

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.530
plants and animals have
adapted to climate change,

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:58.490
but there's a few fundamental
different things now

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:01.100
that are very likely to
make it quite difficult.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.160
In addition, it's not just climate change

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:05.680
that's threatening plants and animals,

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.960
it's habitat fragmentation,

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.730
it's pollution,

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:11.780
it's a variety of other
environmental stressors.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.470
And so once you kind of put climate change

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:18.060
on top of pollution,
on top of habitat loss,

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.460
then it becomes much, much more difficult.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.120
And just to add to that,

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.800
I think the extinction rate
of species is much higher

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.690
than the natural extinction rate.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:29.300
And it's partly driven by

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.335
the processes that Ben just mentioned.

00:03:31.335 --> 00:03:33.140
Cook: Myths from social media.

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.190
"Global warming is natural."

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:38.360
So, we know in the past
that climate can change

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.710
really dramatically from natural causes.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:42.510
The climate during the
time of the dinosaurs

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:43.710
is very different from the climate

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:45.440
during the time of the last ice age.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.120
But the changes that
we're seeing right now

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:48.770
for the most part are not natural.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.690
The warming that we're
seeing is very likely

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:52.170
the fastest warming we've seen

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.110
anytime in the last
several thousand years.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:57.650
It coincides directly with
the industrial revolution

00:03:57.650 --> 00:03:59.280
and the burning of fossil fuels

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:00.730
and widespread deforestation.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:03.260
You can look at almost any natural cause,

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:06.490
and none of them are sufficient to explain

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:08.530
the warming that we've
seen in recent decades.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:10.780
"Carbon dioxide is the problem."

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:12.970
So, CO2 isn't the problem.

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:16.132
It's the increase in
the concentration of CO2

00:04:16.132 --> 00:04:18.470
in the atmosphere that is resulting

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:21.087
in the rapid warming we're seeing

00:04:21.087 --> 00:04:23.830
over the last century,
which is the problem.

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.740
So, carbon dioxide is one of these gases

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:27.890
that we call greenhouse gases,

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:29.780
because they're responsible
for the greenhouse effect,

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.420
which basically helps trap energy on Earth

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:34.100
and make things much, much warmer

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:35.270
than it otherwise would have been.

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:37.060
It's not a big stretch then to observe

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:39.410
that if we start increasing
CO2 concentrations,

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:42.750
we're gonna trap more energy
and we're gonna warm up.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:45.110
Before the industrial revolution,

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:47.913
CO2 levels were close to,
like, 280 parts per million.

00:04:47.913 --> 00:04:51.733
And now we're at close
to 418 parts per million.

00:04:51.733 --> 00:04:54.769
So that's a pretty large
change in the concentration.

00:04:54.769 --> 00:04:58.200
And the fact is that pretty much anytime

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.830
the world was warmer,

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:02.530
CO2 levels in the atmosphere were higher.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.020
And anytime the world was cooler,

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:07.260
CO2 concentrations were lower.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:09.900
"A few degrees' difference
is not a big deal."

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.120
And the way I always like
to think about it too

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:12.580
is like your body's temperature.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:16.130
We're all supposed to be
about 98.6 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:18.290
Even one degree or two degrees of warming

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.050
is considered a low-grade fever.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.890
And if your temperature is
three or four degrees warmer,

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.840
then you're seriously sick.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.270
So, just to give you a sense,

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.580
the Earth has warmed by about one degree

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:30.240
over the last century.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:33.650
That one degree is an average
temperature around our planet.

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:35.100
That means some parts of our planet

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.300
are warming faster than others.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:38.270
I come from India.

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:39.495
We have a lot of people

00:05:39.495 --> 00:05:42.090
that live below poverty in the country.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:44.320
And most of those people, for example,

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:45.520
don't have an air conditioner

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.480
to deal with extreme heat events.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.790
So it depends on who we're talking about

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:51.490
when we say it's not a big deal,

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:53.720
because there are some
people around the planet

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:56.430
that have the capacity to adapt or cope

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:58.320
with these kind of extreme events

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.080
and with the warming
that we've experienced,

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:01.890
and then there are billions of people

00:06:01.890 --> 00:06:04.460
that do not have the capacity to cope

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:06.810
with even small changes.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:10.470
Myths that we, climate
scientists, hear the most.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:14.120
"Global warming will
destroy the planet by 2030."

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:16.550
Just like there's kind of climate deniers

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:17.815
who don't know what they're talking about,

00:06:17.815 --> 00:06:19.090
there's climate doomists

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:20.500
who also don't know what
they're talking about.

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:21.890
This whole idea of the planet

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:26.530
being destroyed by 2030
comes out of discussion

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:27.840
about, how much time do we have

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:29.990
to keep global warming under two degrees?

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:31.312
And so it's very likely that we need to

00:06:31.312 --> 00:06:33.060
kind of get emissions
under control by 2030

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.120
to keep it under two degrees.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:37.550
It doesn't mean that the
world is going to explode

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:39.013
or we're all going to be consigned

00:06:39.013 --> 00:06:42.373
to a fiery "Mad Max" kind of hellscape.

00:06:44.389 --> 00:06:45.770
It just means that it'll be warmer

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:47.460
than we maybe wanted it to be.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.322
When they say it's gonna
destroy the planet,

00:06:49.322 --> 00:06:52.020
well, the planet's not going to blow up.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:54.370
But it does mean that the way of life

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:55.595
and the livelihoods

00:06:55.595 --> 00:06:57.311
and the things people depend on

00:06:57.311 --> 00:06:58.930
are going to be affected.

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:01.130
There are already people
who have been displaced

00:07:01.130 --> 00:07:02.563
because of sea-level rise,

00:07:02.563 --> 00:07:04.046
people that are experiencing

00:07:04.046 --> 00:07:06.180
life-threatening heat conditions.

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:07.250
The impacts of climate change

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:08.610
are not going to be equally felt.

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:09.635
These kind of blanket statements

00:07:09.635 --> 00:07:10.830
are very, very dismissive.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:12.171
And I think they can take attention

00:07:12.171 --> 00:07:13.837
away from the people who are likely to be

00:07:13.837 --> 00:07:15.513
most vulnerable to climate change.

00:07:15.513 --> 00:07:17.810
It's not really helpful
to put a date on it.

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:19.720
I think we just need to know that

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:20.940
delaying action on climate change

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.090
is going to just cost more to society.

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:24.860
Cook: "Global warming is China's fault."

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:26.150
So, to address that myth,

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:29.660
I think there's one important
fact we need to understand.

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:31.310
When CO2 is emitted,

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:34.130
it can stay in the
atmosphere for hundreds,

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:35.420
if not thousands of years.

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:37.800
The CO2 concentrations we're seeing today

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.950
are a consequence of
emissions that have happened

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:44.020
over a much longer period,
over the last century.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:46.860
And most of those emissions are associated

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:48.585
with the industrial revolution

00:07:48.585 --> 00:07:51.343
and development of countries like the US

00:07:51.343 --> 00:07:53.830
and industrialized nations in Europe.

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:57.380
If we look at emissions
this year specifically,

00:07:57.380 --> 00:07:59.570
sure, the emissions from China

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:02.200
are close to what the
emissions from the US are.

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:04.291
But those emissions are being used

00:08:04.291 --> 00:08:05.932
to produce products and goods

00:08:05.932 --> 00:08:08.150
that are being used in
other parts of the world.

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:10.608
So I don't think it's fair to say

00:08:10.608 --> 00:08:11.666
that China's responsible

00:08:11.666 --> 00:08:14.320
when we're all benefiting
from the products

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:15.240
that are produced there.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:17.370
I think even today, it's worth thinking

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:20.050
not just about how much
is a country emitting,

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:22.390
but how much are they emitting per person?

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:24.840
And I have another visual aid here.

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:26.360
You can pretty clearly see

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:28.018
the highest-intensity emitters

00:08:28.018 --> 00:08:29.968
are places like Australia,

00:08:29.968 --> 00:08:33.250
the US, Canada, Russia, Saudi Arabia.

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:35.030
China isn't even in the top 10.

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:36.690
It's also a complicated problem

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:38.150
because the well-being of people

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:40.150
is tied to their consumption of energy.

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:41.958
So as long as we're doing that

00:08:41.958 --> 00:08:44.299
in a sustainable, cleaner way,

00:08:44.299 --> 00:08:47.060
I think we all have to benefit from it.

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:50.590
"Renewable energy is too
expensive to be realistic."

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:54.750
Renewable energy is getting
cheaper all the time,

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:57.470
even faster than we expected.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:58.790
And there's a lot of places

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:01.540
where it actually can outcompete
some fossil fuel sources.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:04.098
For example, I believe wind and solar

00:09:04.098 --> 00:09:07.497
is more cost-effective than coal

00:09:07.497 --> 00:09:10.100
in pretty much the entire United States.

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:13.080
The cost of producing solar panels today

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:16.020
is a fraction of what it
was just a decade ago.

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:17.370
I keep going back to India

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:19.140
because that's another region
I'm very familiar with.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:20.540
There are a lot of villages there

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:22.260
that have been provided energy

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:25.400
because they're using solar and wind,

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:26.750
which would not have been possible

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:29.650
if we were still depending upon CO2.

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:31.470
Now, there's still challenges.

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:32.594
We're not going to kind of

00:09:32.594 --> 00:09:35.302
be able to switch everything overnight,

00:09:35.302 --> 00:09:37.020
but it's like any other technology.

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:39.450
It's getting cheaper over time.

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:40.530
It's getting more efficient.

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:42.270
And the more we kind of invest in it,

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:43.950
then the faster we'll get to the point

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:46.900
that we'll be able to use
it for most of our needs.

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:49.520
"Extreme weather isn't
caused by global warming."

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:50.962
So, the right question to ask

00:09:50.962 --> 00:09:52.510
is not whether an extreme event

00:09:52.510 --> 00:09:55.460
would have been possible without warming,

00:09:55.460 --> 00:09:57.820
but it's to ask how the event itself

00:09:57.820 --> 00:09:59.540
was affected by warming.

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:02.840
For example, a tropical
storm or a tropical cyclone

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:06.300
might result in heavier precipitation

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:09.290
because a warmer atmosphere
holds more moisture.

00:10:09.290 --> 00:10:11.760
And so there's more moisture,
more fuel in the storm,

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:13.790
which results in heavier precipitation

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:15.240
and likely more flooding.

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:16.210
I think a good analogy

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:18.030
is a professional athlete on steroids.

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:20.860
Athletes need to have some
kind of innate fitness

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:23.070
and ability, but if you go on steroids,

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:25.070
you're a bit more likely
to hit a home run.

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:26.078
So CO2 is kind of like

00:10:26.078 --> 00:10:27.670
the steroids of the climate system,

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:30.600
and it's just intensifying
everything that's already there.

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:32.890
"The temperature record is unreliable."

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:34.590
What do you have to say about that, Ben?

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:36.620
The record we have of warming
for the last 150 years

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:39.325
is constructed from basically

00:10:39.325 --> 00:10:42.357
thousands of thermometer
records from around the world.

00:10:42.357 --> 00:10:43.805
Climate scientists often get accused

00:10:43.805 --> 00:10:45.950
of modifying the temperature record

00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:48.540
to make it look like it's
warming more than it actually is.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:50.740
At least half a dozen
groups around the world

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:53.640
who are all independently
putting together these records

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:55.870
and estimating the global
temperature changes

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:58.080
that we've seen over
the last two centuries,

00:10:58.080 --> 00:10:59.870
and they're all basically
getting the same answer.

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:01.580
All this data is publicly available!

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:04.140
Anybody can go and get this data

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:06.310
and come up with their own calculation.

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:09.900
And the fact is that nobody has shown one

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:12.010
that is credibly different.

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:14.260
"It's too late to do anything about it."

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:15.640
It's easy for us to say,

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:16.990
"Well, it's too late to
do anything about it.

00:11:16.990 --> 00:11:19.650
Let's throw our hands up and
not do anything about it."

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:23.300
But there is a lot we can do
about it, both individually

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:25.820
as well as at the international level.

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:27.340
It doesn't have to be a major change,

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:32.130
but reducing our consumption
of certain meat products

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:34.230
that are extremely energy-intensive

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:36.710
is one way in which we can
affect greenhouse emissions.

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:38.180
The decisions we make today,

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:39.490
we are going to have to deal with,

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:40.940
our children are going
to have to live with.

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:44.210
I will never say that
people should not recycle

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:46.650
or reduce their car use or eat less meat.

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:47.880
But at the end of the day,

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:49.670
the big lever is just
going to be government.

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:51.860
And 'cause the government can set policies

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:54.419
that can incentivize actions.

00:11:54.419 --> 00:11:56.035
It's also a weird time to say

00:11:56.035 --> 00:11:57.810
that it's too late to
do anything about it,

00:11:57.810 --> 00:11:59.720
because we're at a point in time

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:02.400
when we have so much information.

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:04.190
There are people working on technologies

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:05.270
to address climate change

00:12:05.270 --> 00:12:07.650
and to make our environment
cleaner and better.

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:09.900
So this is not a time for
us to put our hands up.

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:11.380
It's our time to take action.

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:13.700
Climate change itself is not pass-fail.

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:14.920
Keeping warming to three degrees

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:16.480
is better than four degrees.

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:18.800
Keeping warming to two and a half degrees

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:20.120
is better than three degrees.

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:21.200
Keeping warming to two degrees

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:22.630
is better than all of those things.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:23.520
We're all stuck here,

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:24.980
so we should try to figure out

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:26.730
how we can make it the best planet we can.

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:27.980
Climate change is a global problem,

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:29.950
and it's going to
require a global solution

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:33.553
and people to actually
kind of work together.

